1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,201
<font color="#FFFFFF">AI code assistants like Copilot suggest
completions, but they make mistakes,</font>

2
00:00:05,281 --> 00:00:09,731
<font color="#FFFFFF">and programmers have to catch those mistakes before
they turn into bugs.</font>

3
00:00:09,811 --> 00:00:14,764
<font color="#FFFFFF">One popular idea is to highlight the tokens the
model was least confident about,</font>

4
00:00:14,844 --> 00:00:16,777
<font color="#FFFFFF">so a person knows where to look.</font>

5
00:00:16,857 --> 00:00:23,508
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper asks a sharp question: are those
generation probabilities actually the right thing to highlight?</font>

6
00:00:23,588 --> 00:00:26,905
<font color="#FFFFFF">Through a preregistered study with thirty programmers,</font>

7
00:00:26,985 --> 00:00:32,253
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors compare highlighting low-probability
tokens against a new edit model that</font>

8
00:00:32,333 --> 00:00:35,965
<font color="#FFFFFF">instead predicts which tokens a person is likely to
change.</font>

9
00:00:36,045 --> 00:00:40,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">The edit model wins on speed, precision of edits,
and user preference,</font>

10
00:00:40,448 --> 00:00:44,080
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that generation probabilities alone are not
enough.</font>

11
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:48,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">AI code assistants are now everywhere, but they are
imperfect,</font>

12
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:52,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a wrong suggestion can quietly plant a bug or a
security hole.</font>

13
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:57,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">To catch those mistakes, a programmer first has to
notice them, and that is hard,</font>

14
00:00:57,330 --> 00:01:01,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">because people tend to over-trust automation.</font>

15
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:05,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">The tricky part is that a single code suggestion is
not one decision but hundreds of tiny ones,</font>

16
00:01:05,910 --> 00:01:10,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">one per token.</font>

17
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:14,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">Earlier research on communicating AI uncertainty
was built for single-shot decisions like a diagnosis,</font>

18
00:01:14,490 --> 00:01:18,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it does not obviously carry over to this
token-by-token world.</font>

19
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:22,201
<font color="#FFFFFF">One natural idea is to highlight uncertain tokens,</font>

20
00:01:22,281 --> 00:01:25,466
<font color="#FFFFFF">much like a spell-checker underlines suspect words,</font>

21
00:01:25,546 --> 00:01:29,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the programmer's eye is drawn to the spots that
most need review.</font>

22
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:35,262
<font color="#FFFFFF">The obvious signal to use is the model's own
generation probability: tokens the model</font>

23
00:01:35,342 --> 00:01:37,631
<font color="#FFFFFF">was least sure about get highlighted.</font>

24
00:01:37,711 --> 00:01:42,176
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is an existing strategy, and it even ships in
OpenAI's Playground.</font>

25
00:01:42,256 --> 00:01:49,091
<font color="#FFFFFF">But nobody had really tested whether low
probability actually lines up with where humans need to make edits,</font>

26
00:01:49,171 --> 00:01:52,740
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that gap is exactly what this paper set out to probe.</font>

27
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:57,885
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors ran a preregistered, mixed-methods
study with thirty programmers,</font>

28
00:01:57,965 --> 00:02:03,295
<font color="#FFFFFF">comparing three ways of presenting the same AI code
completions: no highlights at all,</font>

29
00:02:03,375 --> 00:02:08,643
<font color="#FFFFFF">highlights based on generation probability, and
highlights based on a new edit model.</font>

30
00:02:08,723 --> 00:02:13,551
<font color="#FFFFFF">The edit model is the key idea: instead of asking
how confident the model was,</font>

31
00:02:13,631 --> 00:02:17,452
<font color="#FFFFFF">it predicts which tokens a human is actually likely
to change.</font>

32
00:02:17,532 --> 00:02:19,465
<font color="#FFFFFF">This reframes the whole problem,</font>

33
00:02:19,545 --> 00:02:23,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">from surfacing model uncertainty to surfacing human
intervention,</font>

34
00:02:23,635 --> 00:02:30,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the authors argue the same recipe could scale
up by learning from edit telemetry that products already collect.</font>

35
00:02:31,170 --> 00:02:35,067
<font color="#FFFFFF">Under the hood, all three tools used the very same
Codex model,</font>

36
00:02:35,147 --> 00:02:39,044
<font color="#FFFFFF">so any difference in behavior comes purely from how
the completion was displayed.</font>

37
00:02:39,124 --> 00:02:43,021
<font color="#FFFFFF">The generation-probability tool highlighted tokens
the model was least confident in,</font>

38
00:02:43,101 --> 00:02:46,998
<font color="#FFFFFF">at a seventy-one percent threshold.</font>

39
00:02:47,078 --> 00:02:50,975
<font color="#FFFFFF">The edit-model tool highlighted tokens most likely
to be edited,</font>

40
00:02:51,055 --> 00:02:54,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">at a sixty-six percent threshold,</font>

41
00:02:55,032 --> 00:02:58,929
<font color="#FFFFFF">using a model trained on nine coders who had
previously edited Codex output until their tasks passed.</font>

42
00:02:59,009 --> 00:03:02,906
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially,</font>

43
00:03:02,986 --> 00:03:06,883
<font color="#FFFFFF">the thresholds were chosen so that every condition
showed the same total number of highlights,</font>

44
00:03:06,963 --> 00:03:10,860
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the comparison is about which tokens get
highlighted, not how many.</font>

45
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:13,086
<font color="#FFFFFF">Thirty participants took part,</font>

46
00:03:13,166 --> 00:03:17,452
<font color="#FFFFFF">all experienced Python programmers at a large US
technology company,</font>

47
00:03:17,532 --> 00:03:21,176
<font color="#FFFFFF">each paid fifty dollars for roughly an hour of their time.</font>

48
00:03:21,256 --> 00:03:25,928
<font color="#FFFFFF">Every person solved three coding problems drawn
from LeetCode's easy tier,</font>

49
00:03:26,008 --> 00:03:27,918
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a ten-minute cap per task,</font>

50
00:03:27,998 --> 00:03:33,119
<font color="#FFFFFF">and could run their code and a set of provided unit
tests to debug along the way.</font>

51
00:03:33,199 --> 00:03:38,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">Task order and which tool went with which task were
randomized to keep the comparison fair.</font>

52
00:03:39,042 --> 00:03:43,970
<font color="#FFFFFF">The edit model itself was trained earlier, on a
separate group of nine coders.</font>

53
00:03:44,350 --> 00:03:46,404
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is about speed.</font>

54
00:03:46,484 --> 00:03:49,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">People finished fastest when using edit-model highlights,</font>

55
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:52,562
<font color="#FFFFFF">at about eight point six minutes on average,</font>

56
00:03:52,642 --> 00:03:57,684
<font color="#FFFFFF">and slowest with generation-probability highlights,
at about nine point six minutes,</font>

57
00:03:57,764 --> 00:04:00,794
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the no-highlight condition sitting in between.</font>

58
00:04:00,874 --> 00:04:05,062
<font color="#FFFFFF">The gap between the two highlighting strategies is
highly significant,</font>

59
00:04:05,142 --> 00:04:07,806
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a p-value of zero point zero zero three.</font>

60
00:04:07,886 --> 00:04:14,208
<font color="#FFFFFF">What makes this striking is that
generation-probability highlighting was actually the worst of the three,</font>

61
00:04:14,288 --> 00:04:17,013
<font color="#FFFFFF">slower even than showing no highlights at all,</font>

62
00:04:17,093 --> 00:04:20,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">so highlighting the wrong tokens can hurt rather
than help.</font>

63
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:24,477
<font color="#FFFFFF">To understand why, the authors tracked which tokens
survived,</font>

64
00:04:24,557 --> 00:04:28,045
<font color="#FFFFFF">meaning they were left unchanged by the participant.</font>

65
00:04:28,125 --> 00:04:31,612
<font color="#FFFFFF">Under the edit model, tokens it left un-highlighted
survived far more often,</font>

66
00:04:31,692 --> 00:04:35,179
<font color="#FFFFFF">about eighty-seven percent of the time,</font>

67
00:04:35,259 --> 00:04:38,746
<font color="#FFFFFF">while tokens it did highlight survived only about
thirty-five percent of the time.</font>

68
00:04:38,826 --> 00:04:42,314
<font color="#FFFFFF">Compare that to generation-probability highlighting,</font>

69
00:04:42,394 --> 00:04:45,881
<font color="#FFFFFF">where highlighted tokens still survived
seventy-four percent of the time.</font>

70
00:04:45,961 --> 00:04:49,448
<font color="#FFFFFF">In other words,</font>

71
00:04:49,528 --> 00:04:53,015
<font color="#FFFFFF">the edit model's highlights closely predict what
people actually change,</font>

72
00:04:53,095 --> 00:04:56,583
<font color="#FFFFFF">while generation-probability highlights barely move
the needle,</font>

73
00:04:56,663 --> 00:05:00,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">and all of these differences are extremely significant.</font>

74
00:05:00,530 --> 00:05:06,718
<font color="#FFFFFF">To put numbers on it: task time dropped from nine
point six one minutes with generation-probability</font>

75
00:05:06,798 --> 00:05:10,897
<font color="#FFFFFF">highlighting to eight point five nine minutes with
the edit model,</font>

76
00:05:10,977 --> 00:05:14,949
<font color="#FFFFFF">a significant difference at p equals zero point
zero zero three.</font>

77
00:05:15,029 --> 00:05:20,078
<font color="#FFFFFF">Highlighted-token survival fell from seventy-four
percent to thirty-five percent,</font>

78
00:05:20,158 --> 00:05:22,991
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing far tighter alignment with real edits,</font>

79
00:05:23,071 --> 00:05:25,587
<font color="#FFFFFF">at p below zero point zero zero zero one.</font>

80
00:05:25,667 --> 00:05:27,993
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on a seven-point preference scale,</font>

81
00:05:28,073 --> 00:05:34,957
<font color="#FFFFFF">users rated the edit highlights three point nine
four versus two point eight eight for generation probability,</font>

82
00:05:35,037 --> 00:05:38,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">significant at p equals zero point zero zero one.</font>

83
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:41,621
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lesson is simple but pointed.</font>

84
00:05:41,701 --> 00:05:44,882
<font color="#FFFFFF">What you highlight matters more than whether you
highlight,</font>

85
00:05:44,962 --> 00:05:48,143
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the right thing to highlight is where people
are likely to edit,</font>

86
00:05:48,223 --> 00:05:51,404
<font color="#FFFFFF">not where the model happened to be unsure.</font>

87
00:05:51,484 --> 00:05:54,666
<font color="#FFFFFF">Generation probabilities alone are not enough.</font>

88
00:05:54,746 --> 00:05:57,927
<font color="#FFFFFF">Encouragingly,</font>

89
00:05:58,007 --> 00:06:01,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">tools like Copilot already log the edits people
make to AI suggestions,</font>

90
00:06:01,268 --> 00:06:04,449
<font color="#FFFFFF">so that same signal could train an open-world edit
model and carry these speed,</font>

91
00:06:04,529 --> 00:06:07,710
<font color="#FFFFFF">precision, and preference gains into everyday coding.</font>
